issue(id="0001" status="closed")
  title Explore lineWidth support for Pug formatting
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers formatter
    i-want line width to improve readability where it is semantically safe
    so-that long Pug files can be normalized without accidentally changing rendered whitespace
  premise.
    Pug is indentation-sensitive and often whitespace-sensitive, so line width is
    not as broadly applicable here as it is in HTML, Markdown, or JavaScript.
    The clearest first use case is multiline prose-like text, especially dotted
    text blocks such as `p.`, `li.`, or `blockquote.` where the author intends a
    text block rather than code. Even there, wrapping must be conservative
    because `script.`, `style.`, `textarea.`, and literal examples inside docs
    often depend on exact line breaks and spaces. A second promising use case is
    width-aware breaking of long attribute lists, because that changes source
    layout more than rendered output. Past that point the value of line width
    gets murky quickly because inline text, piped text, interpolation, and
    significant leading or trailing spaces can all change meaning.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Decide and document which Pug constructs are eligible for line-width-driven wrapping and which are explicitly excluded.
    criterion If multiline text wrapping is implemented, apply it only to prose-like dotted blocks whose content is demonstrably plain and low-risk, including custom-tag blocks, and do not reflow raw-tag blocks such as `pre.`, `script.`, `style.`, or `textarea.`.
    criterion Consider long attribute lists as a separate width-aware formatting rule, with examples showing when attributes should stay inline versus split across lines.
    criterion Thread lineWidth through the dprint plugin and standalone converter configuration only after the supported behaviors are defined.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") The current line-width implementation now reflows plain, low-risk dotted prose blocks, including custom tags, while preserving author hard breaks for raw or otherwise whitespace-sensitive text forms.
    item(status="covered") Piped text lines (`|`), interpolation-heavy inline text, literal HTML text, and code-like dotted blocks remain excluded from line-width-driven wrapping because their whitespace is too semantically risky.
    item(status="covered") The first supported line-width behavior is multiline wrapping for long attribute lists, using the structured attribute model from task `0003`.
    item(status="covered") Attribute quote normalization and structural formatting now provide the stable base for width-aware attribute wrapping.
    item(status="covered") Future dotted prose wrapping should use a hard raw-tag denylist (`pre`, `script`, `style`, `textarea`) plus syntax and content-shape checks, not a prose-tag allowlist; custom tags remain eligible when their dotted content is plainly prose-like.
